to memory we control with function pointers. We control `contents` (file data). If we can point xvec at contents (address unknown due to heap ASLR though). Hmm.\n\nBut wait, the write values come from `contents`, and `contents` is a heap pointer unknown to us. We can't write the address of contents. But the second loop writes `arsym->name = p` where p is a pointer into contents — that's an info leak into symdefs, but not printed.\n\nHmm, hard.\n\nLet me reconsider whether ASLR is actually on for the target. Check /proc/sys/kernel/randomize_va_space. If it's 0, then heap and libc addresses are fixed, and exploitation is much easier! Let me check. Also PIE off. If ASLR disabled, we know libc base from local (same environment) and heap addresses. Let's check.\n\nAlso, maybe there's a much simpler intended path: The binary reads the input file and calls bfd functions. Since it's non-PIE and no ASLR maybe, we can overwrite GOT with system.\n\nLet me check randomize_va_space and glibc version."}}
